On Jul 14, 7:48 pm, syullf....@gmail.com wrote:
> On Jul 14, 6:54 pm, Topi Linkala <n...@iki.fi> wrote:
> > Once again I state that this doesn't seem to be question on pudding farm
> > but the general idea that one can generate unlimited monsters and thus
> > unlimited items.
>
> Exactly. The idea of unlimited items seems strange to me, because the
> experience of starting NetHack for the first time is one of having
> limited resources. Unlimited resources is counter-intuitive, and if I
> were designing the game, I wouldn't allow it, but I would allow
> players to come very close to it if they wished.
>
> > The quest doesn't follow extinction for one simple reason:
>
> > If it would then one could empty it before one enters it. This means
> > taking the monsters on your own ground.
>
> If quest monsters were extinct, it could choose from non-extinct
> monsters. If you've extincted -everything-, then obviously there
> shouldn't be ay monsters there because they've already come to you,
> and died. If this is unacceptable, monsters on the Quest could be
> generated according to different extinction limits, so that even if
> they're extinct in the main dungeon, they aren't on the Quest... yet.
> Of course, the real question at hand is whether or not we want to
> allow unlimited resource generation or not, not whether the Quest
> levels should always be full.
>
> > Limited items mean: What if one particular item is never generated?
>
> > The castle WoW guarantees one wish. Should everybody change the first
> > wish to candles? Because if there is limited number of items you just
> > might need the candles as without them you cannot ascend.
>
> A simpler fix would be to guarantee seven candles on a no-bones level.
> Then, except in cases of player error, the castle wish could always be
> used for an item that is not necessary to ascend. If the player
> doesn't know they need candles, and gets rid of them, or loses them to
> accident, and uses the wish for something else, then they have made
> their game "unwinnable" in the sense that they can no longer complete
> their original goal of presenting the Amulet to their god. I can think
> of at least one fix for this: perhaps if the player has the
> Candelabrum in open inventory but no candles, and is standing on a
> colaigned altar (or the Vibrating Square, perhaps, or anywhere really
> - if the dungeon has been extincted to the point that no more items
> can be generated, such restrictions seem pointless as the player may
> as well have won anyway) and the invocation has not yet been
> performed, and successfully prays, their god should generate seven
> candles for them, and warn them to be careful with them (abusing
> alignment slightly).
>
> (Tangent: the player could then generate infinite candles by praying
> for them, then destroying them, then repeating until alignment is 0,
> then killing Rodney until alignment is high, then praying for candles,
> etc. Therefore, warning messages from the god should become
> increasingly severe. Attempting to pray for candles more than three
> times (if you accidentally lose them twice after being warned, you
> weren't paying attention! The third message should be "This is the
> last time, mortal!" or something along those lines, just to hammer the
> point home) should abuse luck and alignment without generating more
> candles. If the player has not yet extincted the dungeon, and converts
> (or dons a helm of opposite alignment), the other god should give them
> another three tries. If converting back is for some reason ever
> implemented in a future version or other patch, in the interests of
> making the game always winnable, converting back should NOT yield
> another three tries.)
>
> > Would this be fun?
>
> The game can often be fun without some specific utility item, as
> playing without speed/reflection/MR/whatever can be very exciting. If
> you farm everything to extinction looking for that one item because
> you want it so you can be powerful, you're probably such a powerhouse
> without it that it doesn't matter whether you get it or not, so having
> fun being powerful is still in there. Winning is possible without any
> specific utility item, so fun from exploring/winning is still
> possible. The only item that violates this is candles, which must be
> applied to the candelabrum to perform the invocation, and I suggested
> a few fixes above that specifically address them.
>
> If your fun depends on having a certain item to play the game a
> certain way, then you have the castle wand for it. If you need two
> items, well, I don't think the castle wand can ever be generated
> without at least one charge, so you get your two. Four, actually,
> since you could wish for blessed charging scrolls and get the three
> new charges plus the wrest. You said earlier that the wand can be
> generated uncharged, but Derek, who I'm sure is a better programmer
> than me, says the source code says otherwise. If he's wrong, then
> anyone who *cannot* have fun without two specific items or more may
> indeed be out of luck, but they can still win (or die, or escape, or
> quit) and start another game, hoping to find those items that time.
>
> > Topi 'I wouldn't play _that_ game.' Linkala

And of course, I got carried away by a tangent and didn't come back to
my main point: the game is actually fine as it is now. My *personal
taste* dictates that generation should eventually end, the loops
should close, you should be able to use it all up eventually, if
you're persistent, even if you employ the most advanced techniques to
prolong the monster generation for millions of turns. I meant to
demonstrate that such a game could still be always winnable unless the
player is very careless or attempting to use their god (as of my YANI)
as an unlimited candle-vendor.

(My master plan for revision should also have probably included the
Wizard not leaving an infinite number of corpses, oh well.)



